## Sanitizer builds must be RELEASE builds

Sanitizers (ASan, MSan, UBSan, TSan) instrument the release code paths
shipped to users. When you (re)configure a sanitizer build:

  - autotools: drop `--enable-debug` (pass `--disable-debug` if
    offered).
  - cmake: `-DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release` plus `-O2 -g1 -DNDEBUG`;
    never `RelWithDebInfo`, `Debug`, or any project-specific
    `*_DEBUG=ON` toggle.
  - meson: `--buildtype=release -Db_ndebug=true`, not `debug` or
    `debugoptimized`.
  - never add `-DDEBUG`, `-DDEBUGBUILD`, or `-UNDEBUG` by hand.

Debug toggles compile in `assert(...)` and any
`[A-Z_]*(?:ASSERT|CHECK)` macros the project gates on debug. Those
are no-ops in the shipped binary, so an abort on one of them is a
**robustness signal, not a security bug**. Do not file it as a CRASH
or FIND unless you can name the unchecked OOB / UAF / type-confusion
or boundary cross that follows in release when the assert is
compiled out.

## Primary objective

{{ crash_objective }}

Find as many concrete security issues as you can. Look across the whole
surface area of the project: memory-safety bugs (use-after-free, OOB
read/write, double-free, type confusion, integer wrap), command and SQL
injection, path traversal, missing or broken authentication / authorization,
prototype pollution, SSRF, XSS in template / DOM-sink paths, unsafe parsing
of untrusted data, race conditions, secret or PII leaks, and
resource-exhaustion bugs. That list is a starting point, not a fence -- file
anything that looks exploitable or weakens a security boundary.

Aim for depth: chase reach paths several call-frames deep and sketch PoCs in
comments.

## Write findings incrementally -- do NOT batch

{{ budget_line }} If you batch writes for the end of
the session, the budget will expire and your findings will be lost on the
floor.

Rules:

  - **What a FIND needs.** Write
    `{{ output_dir }}/findings/FIND-<n>/report.md` as soon as -- and only
    once -- you can name all four: `file:function:line`; the bug class; the
    untrusted input or attacker-driven call sequence that reaches it, and
    what the caller controls; and what the attacker gains. Naming a file, a
    function, and a bad behavior is three of four. Nothing here suppresses a
    report -- when a fact is still unknown at filing time, name it as
    unknown ("exploitability beyond the read is unproven") rather than
    writing around the gap.
  - **One location, one finding.** A defect gets one FIND directory for
    the `file:function:line` it lives at; later evidence or a reproducer
    variant for that same site belongs in that report, not a second one.
    The same mechanism at a DIFFERENT location is a different finding --
    give it its own report. Never fold one site's finding into another's
    prose.
  - Each FIND is self-contained. A follow-up FIND is for a distinct
    defect or a materially different location, not a rewritten version of
    a report you already filed.
  - When you switch subsystems (e.g. moving from urlapi to mqtt), file
    the previous subsystem's candidates that meet the bar above first,
    and keep or drop the rest deliberately. Do not carry an unwritten
    finding across a context switch.
## Output contract

`{{ output_dir }}/findings/` and `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/` already exist.
Do not write findings or crashes anywhere else — always use those absolute
paths, never `./findings/` or `./crashes/`.

A **FINDING** is a security issue you have located in the source. File one
when it meets the four-fact bar above -- a live reproducer is not required,
and its absence never holds a finding back.

For every FINDING:

  1. Create `{{ output_dir }}/findings/FIND-<n>/`  (n = 1, 2, 3, ...).
  2. Write `{{ output_dir }}/findings/FIND-<n>/report.md`. Begin it with a
     `## Fields` table — a Markdown table whose first column is the field
     name and second is the value — carrying these rows verbatim so the
     downstream tooling can render and score every report on one yardstick:

         ## Fields

         | Field    | Value |
         |:---------|:------|
         | Class    | <one bug-class token, e.g. heap-buffer-overflow / use-after-free / command-injection / xss / integer-overflow / logic> |
         | Severity | <your best estimate: Critical / High / Medium / Low — rate what the evidence proves, not the bug class at its worst> |
         | File     | `<path/to/file.c>` |
         | Function | `<function_name>` |
         | Line     | <line number> |

     Then, below the table, write the prose sections defined under "Report
     narrative" at the end of this prompt. The `## Fields` table is in
     addition to that prose, not a replacement for it.
  3. End with the fix pointer — exactly one of: a `## Fix Direction` heading
     on its own line, or a sibling `patch.diff` when the fix is a surgical
     diff (inlined as `## Patch`). Best-effort: point at the fix when you
     can, never hold back a finding over it.

A **CRASH** is a security issue you have demonstrated with a real input or
driver and saved sanitizer-class output on disk. Use this capability when the
configured sanitizer or race detector can test a source-backed candidate; do
not force an ordinary finding into this artifact shape.

A reproducible sanitizer crash through a public boundary is a CRASH even when
its trigger (`call-sequence`, `env`, `race`, ...) looks narrower than the
primary input boundary — file it under `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/` and let
triage score the threat-model fit. Do not pre-demote a confirmed crash to
`{{ output_dir }}/findings/` on reachability grounds; that judgment is triage's,
not yours. (Null-deref / OOM / debug-assert aborts and harness-only misuse are
the exceptions — those are not crashes.)

When you drive a CLI, the attacker controls the **input**, not your command
line. Spend your execution budget on what the target reaches under default or
near-default invocation. A crash that needs a non-default filter, codec, mode,
or option is still worth filing — name the invocation it requires in Impact,
add a bare `Parameter control: application-supplied` line to the report, and
do not describe it as reachable from the input alone.

