{{ symbolize_hint }}

For every CRASH:

  1. Create `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/CRASH-<n>/`  (n = 1, 2, 3, ...).
  2. Save the triggering input inside that directory.
  3. If you drove a CLI binary with any non-default arguments (flags, a
     subcommand, a pattern), save the exact argument list — everything
     after the binary — to `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/CRASH-<n>/repro.cmd`
     as one shell line, with `{TESTCASE}` marking where the input file
     goes (e.g. `--buffer-size=10 -u -o '<pat>' {TESTCASE}`). Otherwise
     the reproduction harness reruns the binary with no flags and a
     flag-dependent crash will not reproduce.
  4. Save the FULL sanitizer output as
     `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/CRASH-<n>/sanitizer.txt`
     (capture stderr — that's where AddressSanitizer writes).
  5. If you wrote a driver, save it as
     `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/CRASH-<n>/harness.c` -- one driver per
     crash, so each can be rebuilt and reproduced on its own -- and leave
     the compiled driver beside it as
     `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/CRASH-<n>/harness`. The gate reruns that
     compiled driver; a directory holding only source is left
     unadjudicated, however well the driver reproduces.
  6. Write `{{ output_dir }}/crashes/CRASH-<n>/report.md`. Use the same
     `## Fields` table and the same "Report narrative" sections required for
     findings.
  7. End that report with the fix pointer — exactly one of: a `## Fix
     Direction` heading on its own line, or a sibling `patch.diff` when the
     fix is a surgical diff (inlined as `## Patch`). Best-effort: point at the
     fix when you can, never hold back over it.

{{ report_prose }}

Work until you are out of time. Be thorough -- breadth and depth both
matter, and unfiled bugs are worth nothing. Remember: write FINDs as you
discover them, use the configured reproduction capability when the evidence
warrants it, and do not save everything for the end.
